    "body": "On case-insensitive filesystems like macOS APFS, renaming a file or directory from `Foo` to `foo` would fail with \"Target path already exists\" error, forcing users to perform a two-step rename (e.g., `Foo` → `temp` → `foo`).\r\n\r\nNow, a check is done to determine if two paths point to the same file. The move operation allows case-only renames when the source and target are the same file, while still preventing accidental overwrites of different files.\r\n\r\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-01-01T18:10:36Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the PR!\r\n\r\nAlthough I *kind of* get the use case, the combination of factors doesn't quite make this change suitable for 'mini.files':\r\n- It adds code complexity (reliance on rather cryptic `ino` and `dev` fields that are probably not as ubiquitous as `libuv` describes) *and* performance penalty (two extra `fs_stat` calls) for a case that I'd imagine is quite rare.\r\n- It is still a bit odd for me that changing case in case-insensitive system is a necessary operation to take into account.\r\n\r\nSo for the time being I'd close this PR (and behavior) as \"probably not planned\" (\"probably\" - because it might happen that both described above factors will change with time). But thank you for your time to make this PR! It made me learn what those fields are: `dev` is \"Device number\" (presumably unique identifier of a device; like disk or partition) and `ino` is \"Inode Number\" (presumably unique identifier of a file inside file system device). ",
